# Test: can a female read a male while everything is moving?
# The other reading test holds one pair still and points them at each other. This one does
# not hold anything: the bar slides, all five bodies sway, and each female has a male in
# front of her only for a few seconds at a time. It is the same question asked in the room
# rather than on a bench, and it is the only one of the two that can tell you she *missed*
# somebody - a lit male plainly in front of her, and nothing read.
# Nothing is answered, nothing is satisfied and no sound is made: this only watches.
# Set the appetites before you press start, or nothing will move.

0 Before starting, set what each body is asking for. Two buttons on the test page do the usual pair of settings: "males asking O, females wanting P" is the one to use for measuring reading, because no female wants what any male is offering and so none of them ever stops to answer one. "every drive to full" makes everyone want everything, which is the one to use if you want to watch a female actually recognise somebody.
0 If any body is left wanting nothing it will simply stand still all run - a satisfied male never calls and a satisfied female never looks. The page says which ones, in red, before you start and while you run.
0 You press start. Both males begin calling and all three females begin looking, each of them swaying back and forth on the spot.
0 Each male's ring starts sending his identity - ten flashes a fifth of a second each, two seconds in all, then dark for a little over two seconds. Expect a ring to be out about half the time: that is the message, not a fault.
2 The bar works out for itself that somebody is calling and sets off along its travel. Nobody tells it to: it watches the males, and it moves because they are calling. If you stop both males by hand the bar stops too, within a second, and stays where it stands.
0 From here nothing is staged and nothing is aimed. The bar carries the two males back and forth along the whole rail, and each female comes in and out of each male's view as the two of them sway.
5 The first readings start arriving. A female needs one whole burst before she can recognise anything, so she needs a male to stay in her view for at least two seconds, and to have arrived before his burst began.
0 When a female reads somebody her head lights up for five seconds - blue for male 1, red for male 2 - and then goes out. That is the readout of this test: a head lighting up means she just recognised the male whose colour it is. During the run her head means that and not how hungry she is.
0 Every reading is written down as it happens, with what she read, which male was actually lined up on her at that moment, and whether the two agree. Nothing is sampled once a second here - only things that actually happened are recorded.
0 One caution on the "expected" column. The burst she decoded was sent up to four and a third seconds before she reported it, and in that time the bar has moved. So on a fast crossing the male named as being in view may not be the male she actually heard. A wrong reading right at the edge of a crossing is more likely to be this than a bad decode.
0 When a female has a lit male in front of her for nearly nine seconds and reads nothing at all, that is written down as a miss. Nine seconds is two whole send cycles - long enough that she cannot have simply arrived in the gap between two bursts. This is the number the test exists for: the other reading test can never produce it, because it holds the pair still until she succeeds.
0 A run of misses on one female and not the others points at that female - her aiming, her sensor, or her resting direction. Misses spread evenly across all three point at the room: the light level, or the males being too far away at the far end of the rail.
0 If you set every drive to full, a female will sooner or later recognise a male asking for something she is short of, and her search will end itself. The test writes that down as a find and immediately starts her looking again, because what would normally follow a find is the answering, and that is not built. So the piece never gets past recognition here, on purpose.
0 The page keeps a running count - read correctly, read wrong, missed, found - and beneath it the last few minutes of events newest first, with the ones that were not clean reads tallied by what they said. That tally is the thing to read: one sentence coming back over and over is systematic, and a scatter of different ones is a poor view.
0 Warning: this test moves everything, so do not run it while the installation itself is running, and do not run it alongside the other reading test - that one carefully lines up one pair and this one will slide the bar straight out from under it.
0 Pressing stop ends it: every male stops calling, every female stops looking, the bar stops wherever it happens to be, and all three heads go out. The bodies are left where they stand rather than being sent home.
